    "q-46837": {
      "dom_id": "step-q46837",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{3-\\sqrt{3x}}{x-3}.$",
      "worked_solution": "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{3-\\sqrt{3x}}{x-3} & =\\frac{3-\\sqrt{3(3)}}{3-3}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{3-\\sqrt{3x}}{x-3} & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{(3-\\sqrt{3x})(3+\\sqrt{3x})}{(x-3)(3+\\sqrt{3x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{{3}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{3x})}^{2}}{(x-3)(3+\\sqrt{3x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{9-3x}{(x-3)(3+\\sqrt{3x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{-3(x-3)}{(x-3)(3+\\sqrt{3x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{-3}{3+\\sqrt{3x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{3+\\sqrt{3\\cdot 3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
