    "q-338517": {
      "dom_id": "step-q338517",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{\\sqrt{x+1}-2}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=0$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{\\sqrt{x+1}-2} & =\\frac{{3}^{2}-9}{\\sqrt{3+1}-2}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{\\sqrt{x+1}-2} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{({x}^{2}-9)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)}{(\\sqrt{x+1}-2)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)}{(\\sqrt{x+1})^{2}-{2}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)}{(x+1)-4} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{(x-3)(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)}{x-3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-3)}(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2)}{\\cancel{x-3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\,(x+3)(\\sqrt{x+1}+2) \\\\  & =(3+3)(\\sqrt{3+1}+2) \\\\  & =6(4) \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
