    "q-46839": {
      "dom_id": "step-q46839",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{x-8}{4-\\sqrt{2x}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=8$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{x-8}{4-\\sqrt{2x}} & =\\frac{8-8}{4-\\sqrt{2\\cdot 8}}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{x-8}{4-\\sqrt{2x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{(x-8)(4+\\sqrt{2x})}{(4-\\sqrt{2x})(4+\\sqrt{2x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{(x-8)(4+\\sqrt{2x})}{{4}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{2x})}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{(x-8)(4+\\sqrt{2x})}{16-2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{(x-8)(4+\\sqrt{2x})}{-2(x-8)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {8}^{+}}\\frac{4+\\sqrt{2x}}{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+\\sqrt{2(8)}}{-2} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
