    "q-46830": {
      "dom_id": "step-q46830",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{x-4}{\\sqrt{3x-3}-3}.$",
      "worked_solution": "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=4$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{x-4}{\\sqrt{3x-3}-3} & =\\frac{4-4}{\\sqrt{3(4)-3}-3}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{x-4}{\\sqrt{3x-3}-3} & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{(x-4)(\\sqrt{3x-3}+3)}{(\\sqrt{3x-3}-3)(\\sqrt{3x-3}+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{(x-4)(\\sqrt{3x-3}+3)}{{(\\sqrt{3x-3})}^{2}-{3}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{(x-4)(\\sqrt{3x-3}+3)}{3x-3-9} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{(x-4)(\\sqrt{3x-3}+3)}{3x-12} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{(x-4)(\\sqrt{3x-3}+3)}{3(x-4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{3x-3}+3}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3(4)-3}+3}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3+3}{3} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
